      ":question/id": "a4285b17-ecc7-566b-a1ef-e6253960cb69",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Given $f(x)=4x^{2}-1$ and $x\\ne 0$, calculate the integral.\n\n$\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{f(x)}{x^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+C.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Using the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{f(x)}{x^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x &= \\int \\frac{4x^{2}-1}{x^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=\\int \\left(4 - \\frac{1}{x^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=4\\int 1\\,\\mathrm{d}x-\\int x^{-2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=4\\left(\\frac{x}{1}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{x^{-1}}{-1}\\right)+C \\\\ &=4x+\\frac{1}{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe blank excludes the constant $C$, which is already supplied.",
